S|               EXT. STREET  DAY
O|
G|               MEDIUM SHOT
O|
N|               George runs down the street until he comes opposite a two-
N|               story building with a sign on it reading: "Bailey Building 
N|               and Loan Association." He stops. Potter's carriage is waiting 
N|               at the entrance. Suddenly he runs up the stairs.
O|
S|               INT. OUTER OFFICE BLDG. AND LOAN  DAY
O|
G|               FULL SHOT
O|
N|               The offices are ancient and a bit on the rickety side. There 
N|               is a counter with a grill, something like a bank. Before a 
N|               door marked:
O|
N,I|               "PETER BAILEY, PRIVATE", George's Uncle Billy stands, 
N|               obviously trying to hear what is going on inside. He is a 
N,I|               very good-humored man of about fifty, in shirt-sleeves. With 
N,I|               him at the door, also listening, are Cousin Tilly Bailey, a 
N,I|               waspish-looking woman, who is the telephone operator, and 
N,I|               Cousin Eustace Bailey, the clerk.  The office vibrates with 
N|               an aura of crisis as George enters and proceeds directly 
N|               toward his father's office.
O|
G|               CLOSE SHOT
O|
